  "title": "What is typology?",
  "aliases": [
    "typology question",
    "typology in the bible",
    "typology overview"
  ],
  "summary": "Typology is the Bible's pattern of earlier persons, events, and institutions that God shapes to point forward to fuller fulfillment in Christ.",
  "historical_context": "Israel's story repeatedly reuses earlier patterns, and the New Testament identifies those patterns as divinely intended preparations.",
  "ancient_near_east_context": "Ancient readers expected stories to echo and recur, but biblical typology gives recurrence theological direction toward Christ.",
  "literary_context": "Typology links exodus, temple, kingship, priesthood, and resurrection by showing how earlier forms anticipate later realities.",
  "covenantal_significance": "Typology demonstrates continuity between covenants without collapsing their differences.",
